WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
INTRO:
I’m sure you all know about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:08.520
Nile River. It is disputedly the longest river in&nbsp;
the world and has been the lifeline for growth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:14.700
development across northeast Africa. The country&nbsp;
that is by far the most dependent on the Nile is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.300
Egypt where 95% of the country’s 102 million&nbsp;
people live along the river banks. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:27.960
Egypt’s population is so concentrated along the&nbsp;
Nile that only about 4% of the country’s land is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.200
actually used by people. The unforgiving deserts&nbsp;
that surround the Nile have only exacerbated this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.660
situation. Considering this, I don’t think you’d&nbsp;
be surprised to hear that protecting the Nile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.960
is the top priority for Egypt. Over the past&nbsp;
century, they’ve signed dozens of treaties and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:52.620
striked numerous deals to take down any project&nbsp;
that threatens Egypt’s use of the Nile. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.620
project that they were not able to stop is the&nbsp;
Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam also known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.320
GERD dam. The goal of the GERD dam was to capture&nbsp;
the might of the Nile river to produce enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.740
amounts hydroelectric power. In fact, the dam was&nbsp;
projected to provide 65 million Ethiopians with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.320
affordable electricity. This electricity can then&nbsp;
be used to develop Ethiopia’s service, industrial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.380
and agricultural sectors and lift millions of&nbsp;
Ethiopians out of poverty. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.200
that sounds like a good thing. How could&nbsp;
such a wholesome project be a negative. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.340
the truth is that it’s not a negative atleast not&nbsp;
for Ethipia, but the same cannot be said about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.880
Egypt. You see, the Nile river doesn’t flow from&nbsp;
North to South as you might intuitively expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.940
The Nile river actually flows south to north&nbsp;
starting in Lake Victoria located in modern day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.280
Uganda, Tanzania, and Kenya and emptying&nbsp;
into the Mediterranean Sea. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.560
that Egypt actually gets the tailend of the&nbsp;
river and that any operations upstream can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.180
affect how much water Egypt receives.&nbsp;
And as you probably already guessed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:09.900
the GERD dam is one such project. So, here’s the&nbsp;
story of the dam that is threatening to end Egypt.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.360
GERD DAM:&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.640
Taking a look back, while the GERD dam didn’t&nbsp;
become a hot topic till the 2010s, the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:27.060
has actually been discussed for nearly 70 years.&nbsp;
In fact, the US Bureau of Reclamation identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:34.260
the site fo the GERD dam somewhere between 1956&nbsp;
and 1964. But, work on the dam never got started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.380
due to a slew of political issues. First of all,&nbsp;
Ethiopia was under the control of an emperor named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.960
Haile Selassie. Selassie was a controversial&nbsp;
leader who had both good and bad intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.660
One one side, he introduced the country’s first&nbsp;
written constitution, he abolished slavery, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.540
tried to defend Ethiopia from an Italian invasion&nbsp;
though he unsuccessful at doing so. On the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.420
side, however, he was also known for ruling with&nbsp;
an iron first and doing whatever it took to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.760
control. For example, he was known for suppressing&nbsp;
rebellions and pushing the boundaries when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.580
to human rights. And let’s just say, there were&nbsp;
a lot of rebellions especially from the princes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:20.520
of Ethiopia often referred to as the Mesafint.&nbsp;
The Mesafint felt that Selassie’s reforms were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:25.620
simply not good enough and that Ethiopia could be&nbsp;
modernizing way faster if they were in control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.860
Ironically, if they simply shut up, Ethiopia&nbsp;
might’ve modernized faster because Selassie was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.500
spending a significant amount of time just keeping&nbsp;
the Mesafint in check. This issue just became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:43.740
worse with time as the rebellions would eventually&nbsp;
boil over and become a military coup that resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:50.640
in Selassie being assassinated in August of 1975.&nbsp;
That wasn’t the end of it either. It was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.540
just the beginning of a 17 year long civil war&nbsp;
that would take Ethiopia into the 1990s. With all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.780
this drama going on, building a dam simply wasn’t&nbsp;
a top priority for anyone. Establishing stability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:09.240
and peace was simply a far greater concern, and&nbsp;
fortunately, Ethiopia would eventually reach some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.980
level of this by the turn of the century. At&nbsp;
this point, they could finally start investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.840
into the country and try to turn things around&nbsp;
for the better, and the GERD dam was of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.900
one of these initiatives. Building the dam was&nbsp;
by no means a fast process though. The Ethiopian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:30.000
government would survey the land for several&nbsp;
months between October of 2009 and August of 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.280
and the design of the dam would be finalized a&nbsp;
few months later. With initial planning out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.320
the way, the Ethiopian government would award an&nbsp;
Italian company named Salini Impregilo now known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:47.460
as Webuild with the contract to build the dam.&nbsp;
The contract was worth $4.8 billion and this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:52.740
by no means an easy amount to put together.&nbsp;
For perspective, when this dam was approved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:59.520
the cost was over 10% of the country’s GDP. So,&nbsp;
funding the dam was very much a national effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.340
for a full decade. The country sold bonds to their&nbsp;
citizens and persuaded employees to contribute a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.440
portion of their paycheck to the dam. Ethiopia&nbsp;
also received international help from China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.360
and some gulf countries. Construction&nbsp;
of the dam is actually still underway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.720
and it’s not expected to be completed till&nbsp;
2023. But, most of the dam has been completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:28.200
and Ethiopia started filling it up in July of 2020&nbsp;
which is when the controversy reached new heights.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.760
ETHIOPIA’S PERSPECTIVE:&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:39.180
From Ethiopia’s perspective, the GERD dam is not&nbsp;
only a development project or an infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:46.440
project. The GERD dam is a natural right. Here’s&nbsp;
the thing, it’s not like Ethiopia is crossing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.960
Egypt’s borders and directly stealing their&nbsp;
water. In fact, Ethiopia doesn’t even share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:58.440
a border with Egypt, they’re actually separated&nbsp;
by Sudan. Not to mention, the blue nile literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:04.620
originates from Lake Tana which is located in&nbsp;
Ethiopia. So, it makes no sense to Ethiopia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:10.560
that Egypt is trying to tell them what they can&nbsp;
and cannot do with their own natural resources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.380
That would be like Abu Dhabi telling Saudi Arabia&nbsp;
to chill out on the oil production so that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:22.140
can keep oil prices high. If anything, Egypt&nbsp;
should only have the power to put in a polite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:27.540
request that Ethiopia consider how the dam would&nbsp;
affect nearby countries and their people. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.320
even if Egypt was being polite about it&nbsp;
instead of trying to control the situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.900
it’s not like Ethiopia has another option. It’s&nbsp;
not like they’re building a vanity project like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.940
the Burj Khalifa or the Jeddah Tower. If&nbsp;
this was the case, Egypt could argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.620
the welfare of Egyptians is more important&nbsp;
that the frivolous luxury of Ethiopians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:54.600
but this is not the case. Ethiopia is building&nbsp;
the GERD dam out of desperate need. Ethiopia is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.600
one of the poorest countries in the world coming&nbsp;
in at rank 170 when it comes to GDP per capita,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:09.060
and that’s with the dam. Before the dam project,&nbsp;
Ethiopia’s GDP per capita was literally $120. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.280
it’s not like Egypt is killing it and is a&nbsp;
developed country either. But, Egypt has arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:21.360
done far better than Ethiopia. Their current GDP&nbsp;
per capita stands at $3,500 which is nearly 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:28.080
times Ethopia. So, when Ethiopia is given a choice&nbsp;
between Ethopian’s welfare and Egyptian’s welfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.560
the choice is obvious. It’s not even a question&nbsp;
which country is in a more desperate state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:42.720
Literally over 90% of Ethiopians have been living&nbsp;
in abject poverty for decades. So, it’s about time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.840
that this finally changes. We should also note&nbsp;
that Ethiopia has been as cautious as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:55.860
every step of the way. After all, they’re goal is&nbsp;
not to screw over Egypt. Their goal is to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.840
advance their own country and they’re more than&nbsp;
willing to try to minimize the damages to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.520
countries. A perfect example of this is their&nbsp;
approach when it comes to filing up the dam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:12.120
itself. Ethiopia has carefully timed each filling&nbsp;
to take advantage of the Kiremt season which takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.880
place between June and September. This is when&nbsp;
rainfall is at its peak meaning that Ethopia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:23.220
can fill up their dam with the minimum amount of&nbsp;
damage. Not to mention, Ethiopia is spreading out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:29.400
the fills between 4 to 7 years. So, there’s little&nbsp;
doubt that Ethiopia is approaching the dam as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:37.260
slowly and methodologically as possible. Really,&nbsp;
the only way Ethiopia could be more careful is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:42.180
just not do the project at all which is obviously&nbsp;
not something that they’re willing to do.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:46.860
EGYPT’S PERSPECTIVE:&nbsp;

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:53.640
Hearing Ethiopia’s perspective, it may seem like&nbsp;
Egypt has a hard case to defend, but it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:59.100
that simple. From Egypt’s perspective, they have&nbsp;
literally given up everything to defend the Nile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:05.820
And this doesn’t just apply to the GERD dam. Egypt&nbsp;
has defended the Nile for centuries and they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:12.240
sacrificed so much for the river. Not to mention,&nbsp;
they literally have legal rights to oversee and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:18.480
influence upstream activities. This legal right&nbsp;
was agreed upon way back in 1929 when the Nile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.060
Waters agreement was signed by Egypt and Great&nbsp;
Britain. At the time, Great Britain represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:31.200
Uganda, Kenya, Tanzania, and Sudan. This treaty,&nbsp;
however, did not include Ethiopia. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:37.980
Ethiopia really wasn’t even considered. So, while&nbsp;
Egypt has the legal right to veto upstream Nile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:43.260
projects, Ethiopia argues that this right only&nbsp;
extends to countries that were involved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:49.440
treaty. Why should Ethiopia abide by a treaty that&nbsp;
they weren’t even involved in. This wasn’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:56.160
only treaty that Ethiopia was left out of either.&nbsp;
In 1959, Egypt and Sudan formed an agreement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:02.640
split the Nile water. Egypt had rights to 55.5&nbsp;
billion cubic meters of Nile water and Sudan had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.760
rights to 18.5 billion cubic meters of Nile water.&nbsp;
So, once again from Egypt’s perspective, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.760
literally have the legal right to x cubic meters&nbsp;
of water. And when Ethiopia’s activities threaten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:20.460
that supply, they feel that they have the right to&nbsp;
fight against Ethiopia. At the same time though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:25.740
Ethiopia wasn’t even involved in the discussions,&nbsp;
so why should they be forced to follow any of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.540
treaties. But, Ethiopia can only pull out&nbsp;
the “we weren’t even involved card” on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:35.160
two agreements because they were very much&nbsp;
involved in the Cairo cooperation framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:42.180
of July 1993. This was an agreement directly&nbsp;
between Egypt and Ethipia. In this agreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.160
“Both countries pledged not to implement water&nbsp;
projects harmful to the interests of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.800
and consult over projects to reduce waste and&nbsp;
increase the flow of waters.” While Ethiopia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.980
had deniability with the other agreements, this&nbsp;
one is as clear as day. Ethiopia literally agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:04.740
to make decisions together with Egypt, but when&nbsp;
it came to the GERD dam, it seems like they took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:11.400
a solo approach. I think you can start to see why&nbsp;
Egypt thinks that Ethiopia is in the wrong. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.700
Egypt doesn’t just have a legal basis against&nbsp;
Ethiopia but also a humanitarian one. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:23.700
Egypt is more well off than Ethiopia economically,&nbsp;
this doesn’t mean that they’re in a position where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:30.780
they can negotiate on the Nile. 97% of Egypt’s&nbsp;
fresh water comes from the Nile. And the Nile’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:36.480
water flow has already been on a decline over the&nbsp;
past several decades. Combine this with Egypt’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.460
rapidly growing population, and you’re left in&nbsp;
a position where Egypt is facing an annual water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:47.280
deficit of 7 billion cubic meters. In fact,&nbsp;
at the current deficit, Egypt could run out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:53.880
of water by 2025. Considering this, who actually&nbsp;
has the greater natural right to the Nile. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:59.580
Ethiopia where much of the water originates or is&nbsp;
it Egypt where much of the water is actually used?

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.260
THE FIGHT FOR THE NILE:
The fight between Ethiopia and Egypt is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:13.380
very much an ongoing situation and is extremely&nbsp;
fluid. By the time the GERD dam is filled up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:20.220
some studies suggest that the Nile’s fresh water&nbsp;
flow to Egypt may be reduced by 25%. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:25.800
the electricity produced by Egyptian dams&nbsp;
may be reduced by ⅓. This would likely take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:31.560
a massive toll on the agriculture that takes place&nbsp;
around the Nile which accounts for 60% of Egypt’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:37.500
food. On the other hand, the GERD dam has already&nbsp;
started to pay off for Ethiopia despite not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:44.700
being complete. I mean, just look at Ethiopia’s&nbsp;
GDP over the past 10 years. The dam was of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:50.700
not the only project contributing to this rise,&nbsp;
but it definitely played a major role. Given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:56.220
the GERD dam will become the largest hydropower&nbsp;
facility in Africa once completed, Ethiopia likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:02.580
has a lot more benefits to reap from the GERD dam,&nbsp;
but that’s only if the rest of it is filled. Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:09.000
do you think is in the right? Egypt or Ethiopia?&nbsp;
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:14.040
hope that both countries are able to sort this out&nbsp;
before the situation becomes irreversible. And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.100
course, consider checking out our discord&nbsp;
